2.3 Expected data structure
The data must contain:

one column identifying the stimuli;
one column identifying the subjects;
several numeric columns corresponding to the sensory attributes.

Each row corresponds to the evaluation of one stimulus by one subject. If subjects evaluate the stimuli over several repetitions or several sessions, each evaluation must appear on a separate row.
Before running the analysis in jamovi, check in the Data tab that:

the variable intended for Stimulus Effect is categorical;
the variable intended for Subject Effect is categorical;
all variables intended for Sensory Attributes are numeric.

3.3 Expected data structure
The data must contain:

one column identifying the stimuli;
one column identifying the subjects;
at least two numeric columns corresponding to the sensory attributes.

Each row corresponds to the evaluation of one stimulus by one subject. If a subject evaluates the same stimulus several times (repetitions or sessions), each evaluation must appear on a separate row.
Before running the analysis in jamovi, check in the Data tab that:

the variable intended for Stimulus Effect is categorical;
the variable intended for Subject Effect is categorical;
all variables intended for Sensory Attributes are numeric.

4.3 Expected data structure
The data must contain:

one categorical column identifying the stimuli;
at least three CATA columns coded as 0/1.

Each row corresponds to the evaluation of one stimulus by one subject. If 40 subjects evaluate 6 stimuli, the file generally contains one row per subject and per stimulus, with one column per CATA term.
Before running the analysis in jamovi, check in the Data tab that:

the variable intended for Stimulus Effect is categorical;
the variables intended for CATA are numeric and contain only 0 and 1;
no CATA response is missing: a missing value is not treated as 0;
at least three CATA terms are selected;
at least three stimuli are actually observed to obtain the two-dimensional factor map.

5.3 Expected data structure
The data must contain:

one categorical column for Stimulus Effect;
one categorical column for Subject Effect;
one numeric column for Liking Variable;
one or more nominal columns for Sensory Attributes.

Each row corresponds to one subject evaluating one stimulus.
Before running the analysis, check that:

Stimulus Effect contains at least two observed products;
Subject Effect contains at least two observed subjects;
Liking Variable is numeric and contains enough finite observations;
JAR variables are defined as Nominal in jamovi;
every JAR attribute contains the category specified in Coding of the JAR level;
every JAR attribute has at least two observed response levels;
the variables used for Stimulus, Subject, Liking, and JAR attributes are distinct.

6.3 Expected data structure
The data must contain:

one optional but strongly recommended column for Stimuli Labels;
at least two pairs of numeric columns in Active (X,Y) Coordinate Pairs;
optionally, numeric pairs in Supplementary (X,Y) Coordinate Pairs.

Each row corresponds to a stimulus. This distinguishes this analysis from other analyses where each row corresponds to an individual response. SEDA requires at least three stimuli. If Stimuli Labels is not completed, the function uses row numbers as stimulus names.
Before running the analysis in jamovi, check in the Data tab that:

Stimuli Labels is categorical if used, with no missing or empty values and one unique label per stimulus;
all active and supplementary coordinates are numeric, complete, and finite;
the number of columns in each coordinate block is even;
no coordinate is selected as both active and supplementary;
each X/Y pair has variability in at least one of its two coordinates.

7.3 Expected data structure
The data must contain:

one optional column for Stimuli Labels;
at least two categorical columns in Active Subjects;
optionally, categorical columns in Supplementary Subjects.

Each row corresponds to a stimulus. This point is important: unlike in other analyses, here a row does not correspond to an individual response, but to a stimulus described by the categorisations provided by several subjects.
Before running the analysis in jamovi, check in the Data tab that:

Stimuli Labels is categorical if you use it;
all columns in Active Subjects are categorical (factors);
all columns in Supplementary Subjects are categorical;
you have placed at least two variables in Active Subjects.

8.3 Expected data structure
The data must contain:

one optional column for Stimuli Labels;
one numeric column for X-axis;
one numeric column for Y-axis;
at least two numeric columns in Liking Variables, one per consumer.

Each row corresponds to a stimulus. The two coordinates describe its position in the 2D space, and the Liking Variables contain the consumers’ scores for that same stimulus.
Before running the analysis in jamovi, check in the Data tab that:

X-axis and Y-axis are two different numeric variables and both vary across stimuli;
all Liking Variables are numeric and are different from the coordinate variables;
at least two liking variables are selected;
if Stimuli Labels is used, labels are non-missing and unique.

SEDA requires at least 4 stimuli, but the minimum then depends on the selected model: 4 for Vector, 5 for Circular, 6 for Elliptical, and 7 for Quadratic. Consumer classification requires at least 3 consumers. Isolated missing liking values can be imputed internally from product and consumer effects; however, an entirely missing consumer, a stimulus with no liking scores, or a consumer with no liking variation is rejected.
